    "result_text": "All 58 test classes pass and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` feature for the owner-create/read flow in this Spring PetClinic app.\n\n**What `riskFlag` means:** a derived-on-read boolean on an owner that is `true` when *any* of three conditions hold, else `false`:\n1. **Possible duplicate** \u2014 the owner's stored `possibleDuplicate` flag is true (soundex(lastName)+postcode soft match).\n2. **Disposable-adjacent email domain** \u2014 the email's domain is a *subdomain* of a known disposable-email domain (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`). Exact disposable domains are already hard-rejected with 400 at create time, so only subdomains survive to be flagged here.\n3. **Over soft capacity** \u2014 the owner's stored `capacityWarning` flag is true (the city already held 40+ owners at create time, i.e. over the soft-capacity/warning threshold below the hard limit of 50).\n\n**Changes made:**\n- `src/main/resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 added the `riskFlag` boolean property (readOnly) to the Owner schema, which generates `OwnerDto.getRiskFlag()`. Documented the exact rule.\n- `src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 added a `@Mapping` for `riskFlag` and two default methods (`deriveRiskFlag`, `isDisposableAdjacent`) plus the disposable-domain set, following the existing \"derived on read\" pattern (like `ownerSegment`).\n\nI chose to derive `riskFlag` on read in the mapper because all three inputs are already available from the persisted owner (`possibleDuplicate`, `capacityWarning`, `email`) \u2014 no schema change or new persisted column was needed.\n\n**Verification:** The new `Cp58Tests` (riskFlag false for a normal owner) passes, along with the related duplicate/capacity/email tests (`Cp10`, `Cp35`, `Cp38`), and the **full suite of all 58 test classes is green** (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures / 0 errors).\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec term \"disposable-adjacent\" wasn't defined anywhere in the repo, and no test exercises the *true* case for it. I interpreted it as \"a subdomain of a blocklisted disposable domain\" \u2014 the most natural reading given the existing exact-match blocklist \u2014 and documented that precisely in the OpenAPI description so the code and spec stay aligned.",
